  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 8,
    "lesson_title": "Decaying Oscillatory Responses",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert left-half-plane complex-conjugate pole locations into exponentially decaying sinusoidal response terms and their BIBO-stable causal LTI interpretation.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Decaying Oscillatory Responses",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Decaying Oscillatory Responses",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Recognize the target cue first: a complex-conjugate pole pair with negative real part is the signature of a decaying oscillatory mode in a causal LTI response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the rule directly: for poles $s=\\sigma\\pm j\\omega$, convert to a causal term template $h_c(t)=e^{\\sigma t}(A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\sin(\\omega t))u(t)$ and treat $\\sigma$ as the growth/decay exponent and $\\omega$ as the oscillation frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{\\omega}{(s+\\alpha)^2+\\omega^2}\\right\\}=e^{-\\alpha t}\\sin(\\omega t)u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h_c(t)=e^{-\\alpha t}(A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\sin(\\omega t))u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the base case now: for $s=-2\\pm j3$, the converted causal mode is $e^{-2t}(A\\cos(3t)+B\\sin(3t))u(t)$, which decays because the exponent is negative. In contrast, $s=-2$ maps to a non-oscillatory decay $e^{-2t}u(t)$, while $s=\\pm j3$ gives sustained oscillation with no exponential decay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Close with this stability check: in a causal LTI mode decomposition, all such conjugate pair terms must satisfy $\\Re\\{s\\}<0$ for BIBO stability; any mode with zero or positive real part is the point where the response fails to decay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Decaying Oscillatory Responses",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying Decaying Oscillations from Left-Half-Plane Pole Pairs",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given pole pairs $s=-2\\pm j3$ and $s=+2\\pm j3$, classify each as decaying oscillatory or not.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this as a mode-identification pass. A pair is a decaying oscillatory contribution when it satisfies three conditions at once: it is complex (nonzero imaginary part), the two values are conjugates of each other, and the common real part is negative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with $s=-2\\pm j3$. This pair is complex because it has $\\omega=\\pm 3\\neq 0$, the values are conjugates, and the real part is $\\sigma=-2$, which is negative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=-2\\pm j3 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\sigma=-2<0,\\;\\omega=3\\neq 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{decaying oscillatory}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=+2\\pm j3 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\sigma=+2>0,\\;\\omega=3\\neq 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{unstable (growing) oscillatory contribution}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the correct classification is: the pair $s=-2\\pm j3$ is a decaying oscillatory mode, while $s=+2\\pm j3$ is an unstable oscillatory mode for a causal LTI decomposition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Decaying Oscillatory Responses",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Reading Decay Rate and Oscillation Frequency from Complex Poles",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For poles $s=-3\\pm j2$, compute the decay rate and frequency used in the time-domain prototype.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same cue as in the prior sections: a complex-conjugate pair is written as $s=\\sigma\\pm j\\omega$ and maps directly to a causal mode template. First identify the two parameters before applying any simplification.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the given poles, read the real and imaginary parts directly: $s=-3\\pm j2$ already matches $\\sigma\\pm j\\omega$ with $\\sigma=-3$ and $\\omega=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=-3\\pm j2 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\sigma=-3,\\; \\omega=2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The decay rate is the magnitude of the negative real part, so the exponential envelope is $e^{-3t}$. The angular frequency is the magnitude of the imaginary coefficient, so the oscillation frequency is $\\omega=2$ radians per second.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h_c(t)=e^{-3t}\\left(A\\cos(2t)+B\\sin(2t)\\right)u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result: decay constant $3$, frequency $2$, and the corresponding prototype mode is $h_c(t)=e^{-3t}(A\\cos(2t)+B\\sin(2t))u(t)$, which is a decaying oscillatory contribution in a causal decomposition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Decaying Oscillatory Responses",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing the Response Term from a Complex-Conjugate Pole Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Poles at $s=-2\\pm j5$ appear in the factor $(s+2)^2+25$. Write the corresponding response mode.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the response-construction cue: once the pole pair is identified, write the denominator in standard causal-decay form and substitute directly into the real-time template.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by matching the provided factor to $(s+\\alpha)^2+\\omega^2$ so that the pair is in template-ready form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(s+2)^2+25 = (s+\\alpha)^2+\\omega^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read off the parameters from this match: $\\alpha=2$ and $\\omega=5$. The conjugate poles are therefore $s=-2\\pm j5$, so the mode is in the decaying oscillatory causal family.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h_c(t)=e^{-2t}\\left(A\\cos(5t)+B\\sin(5t)\\right)u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: the response mode is $h_c(t)=e^{-2t}(A\\cos(5t)+B\\sin(5t))u(t)$, and because $\\alpha=2>0$ in this course's convention, the exponential envelope decays in time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Decaying Oscillatory Responses",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting BIBO Stability from Decaying Oscillatory Modes",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Determine stability from poles corresponding to decaying terms $e^{-2t}(A\\cos 4t)u(t)$, $e^{-t}(B\\sin 3t)u(t)$, and one growing oscillatory mode $e^{t}(C\\cos 2t)u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the same per-mode cue used in this topic: a causal mode is stable only if each exponential envelope decays for $t \\to +\\infty$. Start by listing the real exponents in the three mode terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h_1(t)=e^{-2t}(A\\cos 4t)u(t),\\; h_2(t)=e^{-t}(B\\sin 3t)u(t),\\; h_3(t)=e^{t}(C\\cos 2t)u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the BIBO rule from the lesson core move: list the real parts of each exponent. For $h_1(t)$ the real part is $-2$, for $h_2(t)$ it is $-1$, and for $h_3(t)$ it is $+1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Re\\{s\\}_{h_1}=-2,\\; \\Re\\{s\\}_{h_2}=-1,\\; \\Re\\{s\\}_{h_3}=+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Aggregate the sign check across all modes: BIBO stability in this causal LTI setting requires every real part to be negative. One positive real part ($+1$) violates the all-negative rule and dominates the combined response behavior.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{BIBO stable} \\iff \\forall \\text{ modes },\\; \\Re\\{s\\}<0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Conclusion: the system is unstable, because the $e^{t}(C\\cos 2t)u(t)$ mode grows over time and breaks bounded-output stability regardless of the other two decaying oscillatory modes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
